EXT. FISHING BOAT "MARGARET JEAN" - DAWN

grey. flat. the engine ticking over.

NELL, 30s, sits on a bait crate scrolling a phone. Her phone. No signal. Her brother TOBY, 30s, coils rope by the winch while their mother ROSALIND, 60s, leans in the wheelhouse door with a mug, and their dad's old deckhand, a man in a yellow slicker none of them have met before, stands at the rail.

Silence.

NELL
so who's he again

TOBY
dad's guy. from before.

ROSALIND
Nobody calls me back about the boat, and then somebody turns up.

STRANGER
I'm just here for the nets. Don't mind me.

Rain. Light. Then more of it.

NELL
we're not selling by the way

TOBY
no one said selling

NELL
mum said selling

ROSALIND
I said "if".

Toby drops the rope, Nell stands, Rosalind sets down her mug on the winch and the stranger takes one step back, all of them tense in the little space between the net and the rail and the thing nobody's saying.

Nothing.

STRANGER
Look, I'm Owen. Owen Pruett. Your dad hired me in 'ninety-eight. He'd want me here.

OWEN
He told me if it came to it I should tell you where he hid the deed.

NELL
what deed

TOBY
there's a deed?

ROSALIND
Oh for God's sake, Owen.

Her phone. Dead.

Gulls. A wave slaps the hull. Nobody moves.

NELL
(quiet)
where

OWEN
Under the third plank. Forward locker.

Toby looks at Nell. Nell looks at Rosalind. Rosalind looks at the water.

Engine. Rain.

TOBY
(low)
we'll check later

NELL
yeah. later.
